  ],
  "text": "Black pepper was once used to pay rent. Before refrigeration, anything that preserved food or covered the taste of old food was real money. Nutmeg grew on a handful of tiny islands and nowhere else on Earth. The Dutch traded away Manhattan to hold on to one of them. But what reshaped the world was not the spice. It was everyone standing between. Every shipment crossed a dozen middlemen, and each one took a cut. So the same pepper cost a fortune by the time it reached Europe. Which makes the entire age of exploration, underneath, a supply chain problem. Columbus sailed west looking for a shortcut to India, and hit something else entirely. The map of the modern world was drawn by people trying to cut out the middleman. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
